We have

$$
\begin{align*}
\frac{d \sigma \left( z \right)}{d z}
& = \frac{e^{-z}}{\left( 1 + e^{-z} \right)^2} \\
& = \boxed{ \sigma \left( z \right)
\left( 1 - \sigma \left( z \right) \right) } .
\end{align*}
$$

We have

$$
\begin{align*}
\nabla_{\mathbf{\beta}} \ L \left( \mathbf{\beta} \right)
& = \boxed{ \sum_{n = 0}^{N-1}
\left(
\sigma \left( \mathbf{x}^{(n), \top} \mathbf{\beta}
\right)
- y^{(n)}
\right)
\mathbf{x}^{(n)}
} \\
& = \boxed{\mathbf{X}^\top \left( \mathbf{z} - \mathbf{y} \right)} .
\end{align*}
$$

We have

$$
\begin{align*}
\nabla_{\mathbf{\beta}}^2 \ L \left( \mathbf{\beta} \right)
& = \boxed{ \sum_{n = 0}^{N-1}
\sigma \left( \mathbf{x}^{(n), \top} \mathbf{\beta}
\right)
\left( 1 - \sigma \left( \mathbf{x}^{(n), \top} \mathbf{\beta}
\right)
\right)
\mathbf{x}^{(n)}
\mathbf{x}^{(n)^\top}
} \\
& = \boxed{\mathbf{X}^\top \mathbf{Z} \mathbf{X} } .
\end{align*}
$$

Because $\sigma \left( \cdot \right) \in \left[ 0 , 1 \right]$ and $\mathbf{x}^{(n)}
\mathbf{x}^{(n)^\top}$ is a positive semidefinite matrix, we have that $\nabla_{\mathbf{\beta}}^2 \ L \left( \mathbf{\beta} \right)$ is a positive semidefinite matrix.

Therefore, $L \left( \mathbf{\beta} \right)$ is (weakly) concave.

